  "id": "Compound::DB00543 - Gene::6530",
  "verbalization": "The integrated evidence suggests a mechanistic link between Amoxapine and Slc6a2, mediated through Trimipramine. Amoxapine is causally associated with several adverse effects, including Taste Peculiar, Adenitis, and Parotid Swelling, all of which are linked to Trimipramine. Trimipramine, a tricyclic antidepressant, binds to Slc6a2, a gene involved in ion transport and neurotransmitter regulation. This binding is supported by the shared lowest common ancestor (LCA) of Amoxapine and Trimipramine, which is the Organic Heterotricyclic Compound. The consistent pathway through Trimipramine indicates that Amoxapine may influence Slc6a2 via this intermediate, though the exact mechanism of interaction remains unclear. The evidence is robust, but the precise molecular interaction between Amoxapine and Slc6a2 requires further investigation to confirm the direct role of Trimipramine in this process.",
